Source Text Tools
*****************

.[perex]
Do you want to check your articles for mistakes, edit them in bulk, or build an editor that points out exactly where a problem is? Texy gives you a document tree in which every element knows exactly where it sits in the source text. An edit then touches only what you want to change, and the rest of the file stays exactly as it was. It works for Markdown and for Texy syntax alike.


Checking Articles
=================

The blog's articles are stored as `.md` files. We want a tool that finds images without a description, because a reader who cannot see an image would never learn what is in it. We want the message in the form you know from editors and linters, with the file name, line and column:

```
articles/dryer.md:5:1 image without a description
articles/dryer.md:7:10 image without a description
```

```php
use Texy\Nodes;

$markdown = new Texy\Markdown\Markdown;
$source = Texy\Markdown\Markdown::normalize(file_get_contents($file));
$document = $markdown->parse($source);

foreach ($document->find(Nodes\ImageNode::class) as $image) {
	if (($image->alt ?? '') === '') {
		$position = Texy\SourcePosition::fromOffset($source, $image->range->offset);
		echo "$file:$position->line:$position->column image without a description\n";
	}
}
```

Notice three things. We don't convert the text to HTML; we call `parse()`, which only parses it into a tree. Before that, we pass it through `Markdown::normalize()`, which unifies line endings and similar details. And each node's `$range` holds the span of text it came from: `$range->offset` is where it starts and `$range->length` how long it is, both in bytes of the normalized text.

`SourcePosition::fromOffset()` turns a byte position into a line and a column. Both count from one, and the column counts characters, so it matches what your editor shows even when the line contains accented letters.

`find()` returns all nodes of a given kind in the tree, and an image's description is in its `$alt` property. Which nodes you will find in the tree and what properties they have is described in the [node overview |custom-handlers#Node Overview].


Rewriting the Source Text
=========================

The blog has moved to HTTPS, and the articles still contain links to `http://my-blog.com`. A search and replace across the files would also replace the addresses in code samples. The tree, however, knows where the link addresses are, so we change only those:

```php
$source = Texy\Markdown\Markdown::normalize(file_get_contents($file));
$document = $markdown->parse($source);
$rewriter = new Texy\Rewriter($source);

foreach ($document->find(Nodes\LinkNode::class) as $link) {
	if ($link->urlRange === null) {
		continue; // a link whose address is not written in the source text
	}
	$url = substr($source, $link->urlRange->offset, $link->urlRange->length);
	if (str_starts_with($url, 'http://my-blog.com')) {
		$rewriter->replace($link->urlRange, 'https://' . substr($url, 7));
	}
}

file_put_contents($file, $rewriter->getResult());
```

```
We bought the dryer from the [shop](https://my-blog.com/shop) and <https://my-blog.com/recipe>.

The address `http://my-blog.com` in code must stay as it is.
```

Both links changed, the one in square brackets and the one in angle brackets, while the address in code stayed. Reference link definitions, the lines `[name]: http://…`, have no address in the tree, though, so this example leaves them alone. A link `[text][name]` has only the name of the reference in its `$urlRange`.

`Texy\Rewriter` only collects the replacements and performs them all at once in `getResult()`. That is why the ranges from the tree stay valid the whole time, even when the text before them has changed in the meantime. `$rewriter->count()` tells you how many replacements actually change the text, so you only need to write the file when it is greater than zero. The rewriter also guards against two mistakes that you would otherwise only discover in a damaged file:

- Two overlapping replacements cannot both be performed. The rewriter throws `Texy\RewriteException` with the position: `Line 1, column 30: the replacement of 'https://example.com/dryer' overlaps another one.`
- When you also pass the text you expect at that place, it checks it. This protects you against a range from another file or from an older version of the text:

```php
$rewriter->replace($link->textRange, 'the Station Dryer Shop', expected: 'the station shop');
```


Parts of the Syntax
===================

The range of a whole node covers all of its syntax, so for a link that means the brackets, the text and the address. But nodes also remember the ranges of the parts that a tool typically changes separately:

|---
| Node | Property | Part of the syntax
|---
| `LinkNode` | `$textRange` | the link text
| `LinkNode` | `$urlRange` | the address, or the name of a reference
| `LinkNode` | `$syntax` | which syntax the link was written in, for example `link` in Markdown, `phrase/span` for `"text":url` in Texy syntax
| `ImageNode` | `$urlRange` | the image address
| `HeadingNode` | `$textRange`, `$marker` | the heading text and the character it is written with
| `ListItemNode` | `$bulletRange`, `$bullet` | the item's bullet
| `InlineCodeNode` | `$codeRange` | inline code without the backticks
| `CodeBlockNode` and other blocks with literal content | `$codeMap` | where the individual parts of the content lie in the source

For example, to rename the shop in the link text without touching the address:

```php
$rewriter->replace($link->textRange, 'the Station Dryer Shop');
```

```
Bought at [the Station Dryer Shop](https://example.com/dryer).
```

Only the nodes of built-in syntaxes know their parts. A node of your [own syntax |custom-syntax] has only the range of its whole syntax, so a tool can only replace it as a whole.


Replacing a Whole Element
=========================

Sometimes you need to write an element anew, for example with something added. Articles in Texy syntax have images without dimensions, and we want to fill them in from the actual files. We modify a copy of the node, have it printed as Texy source text, and put it in place of the original syntax:

```php
$imageDir = __DIR__ . '/www/images';
$texy = new Texy\Texy;
$document = $texy->parse($source);
$rewriter = new Texy\Rewriter($source);
$printer = new Texy\Output\Texy\Renderer($texy);

foreach ($document->find(Nodes\ImageNode::class) as $image) {
	if ($image->width !== null || $image->height !== null) {
		continue; // the author already wrote the dimensions
	}
	[$width, $height] = getimagesize("$imageDir/$image->url");
	$sized = clone $image;
	$sized->width = $width;
	$sized->height = $height;
	$rewriter->replace($image->range, $printer->renderNode($sized));
}
```

```texy
[* dryer.png .(Dryer in the attic) >] *** Photo: Jane Smith
```

becomes

```texy
[* dryer.png 300x200 .(Dryer in the attic) >] *** Photo: Jane Smith
```

We clone the node so that we don't change the tree we are still reading from. `Texy\Output\Texy\Renderer` is the printer, which writes a node out as Texy source text. It gets the `$texy` object so that it knows how the text will be read again and can escape whatever would otherwise be read as syntax. Syntax that can be written in several forms is printed in the form the author used, for example the same bullet or the same kind of heading. Everything else is printed in one fixed form, so a modifier always comes out as `.(title)[class]{style}` and a code block always between <code>&#96;&#96;&#96;</code> lines.

The printer cannot print a node of your own syntax and throws an exception. You teach it with a renderer registered through `$printer->addRenderer()`, just like a [renderer for HTML |custom-handlers#Different HTML for an Element], except that it returns source text.


Syntax Tree and Document
========================

Why do the tools call `parse()` and not `toHtml()`? Because the tree exists in two states.

- The **syntax tree**, which `parse()` returns, describes how the text is written. The ranges match the syntax exactly, and nothing is filled in.
- The **document** is what you get when the syntax tree goes through the transformations in `transform()`. They give it meaning: they resolve where references lead, even out the heading levels, apply typography. A range then tells you where a node came from, not what is written at that place.

The difference is easy to see on a reference link:

```texy
See "recipe":[recipe].

[recipe]: https://example.com/recipe
```

In the syntax tree, `$link->url` is empty, `$link->ref` is `recipe` and `$link->urlRange` points to `[recipe]`, which is what the text actually says. After `transform()`, `$link->url` holds the address `https://example.com/recipe`, which is not written at that place. A tool that rewrote the text based on the document would thus write the address where the name of the reference was.

When you need both states, for example in a tool that reports mistakes in the resulting HTML too, clone the syntax tree before the transformation: `clone $document` copies the whole tree.


Positions and Line Endings
==========================

Ranges count bytes of the normalized text. For Texy syntax, the text is normalized by `Texy::normalize()`: it converts line endings to `\n` and removes the BOM, control characters, soft hyphens, spaces at the ends of lines and blank lines at the start and end of the text. It keeps the newline that ends the last line, so after a rewrite the file ends just as it did before. Markdown has its own `Markdown::normalize()`, which converts line endings and removes control characters but nothing else, because two spaces at the end of a line are a line break in Markdown. Both reject text that is not valid UTF-8 with a `Texy\InvalidInputException`. That is why a tool normalizes the text once at the start and from then on works only with the result, just like the examples above.

The simplest approach is to save the files already normalized. The file on disk is then identical to the text the positions refer to, and you can use the positions from the tool directly in it.

Process files with Windows line endings (CRLF) in their normalized form and restore CRLF only when writing:

```php
$source = Texy\Texy::normalize(file_get_contents($file));    // CRLF becomes LF
$rewriter = new Texy\Rewriter($source);
// ... replacements based on ranges from the tree ...
file_put_contents($file, str_replace("\n", "\r\n", $rewriter->getResult())); // CRLF again on disk
```

Normalized text contains no `\r`, so the conversion is lossless, and the next time you load the file you get exactly the same text with the same positions. Lines and columns are the same in both forms; only the byte positions differ.


Converting Between Markdown and Texy Syntax
===========================================

Because both languages parse text into the same tree, converting between them is just a matter of which language reads the text and which one prints it. You convert a Markdown article to Texy syntax with a single call:

```php
$markdown = new Texy\Markdown\Markdown;
$texySource = $markdown->toTexy($text);
```

```
# Plum Butter Recipe

- pick
- [wash](https://example.com)
```

```texy
Plum Butter Recipe
##################

- pick
- "wash":https://example.com
```

The content stays the same; only the syntax changes. GFM extensions such as tables are converted only when you read the text with the object from `Markdown::gfm()`. Otherwise they are ordinary text to Markdown. Texy syntax has no task lists, so a check mark stays as the text `[x]`, and strikethrough text is written as the HTML tag `<del>`.

The opposite direction is handled by `Texy\Markdown\Output\Markdown\Renderer`. It takes the document after `transform()`:

```php
$texy = new Texy\Texy;
$document = $texy->parse($text);
$texy->transform($document);

$renderer = new Texy\Markdown\Output\Markdown\Renderer;
$markdownSource = $renderer->render($document);
```

Whatever Markdown cannot express is converted to the closest form it knows. A caption under an image becomes italics on the next line, a table with merged cells becomes an HTML table, and classes, styles, dimensions and alignment of images are lost.

You set the shape of the resulting Markdown with the renderer's properties, for example which character starts bullets or how headings and code blocks are written: `$unorderedListMarker`, `$headingStyle`, `$codeBlockStyle`, `$codeFence`, `$emphasisMarker`, `$strongMarker` and others. The renderer writes addresses just as they are in the tree, even a link to `javascript:`. If you want to filter them, pass it a URL policy in the constructor, as the next section describes.


Printing Back to Texy Syntax
============================

`$texy->renderTexy($document)` prints the whole syntax tree. Parsing the result again yields the same tree, and printing that tree yields the same text again. This is exactly what tools that modify the tree and want to save it rely on.

A few rare constructs do not come back exactly, though: a table cell that continues over more lines than the table rows it spans, a line starting with `/--` or `\--` inside an `html` or `text` block, a modifier title spanning a line break, an empty `/--div` block, the obsolete image syntax `[* a | b *]`, and lines of a raw HTML paragraph that would be read as a block once moved to the start of a line.

Print the syntax tree from `parse()`, not the document after `transform()`. The document already has its references resolved, typography applied and HTML adjusted, so printing it would not give you the text the author wrote.

The printer writes addresses just as they are in the tree. When someone else is going to publish the resulting text and you want to remove dangerous addresses from it, pass a URL policy that rejects them. The default policy of the `$texy` object allows everything, so take, for example, the one from an object in [safe mode |configuration#Safe Mode]:

```php
$safe = new Texy\Texy;
Texy\Configurator::safeMode($safe);
$output = $texy->renderTexy($document, $safe->urlPolicy);
```

A link the policy rejects remains plain text, and an image is reduced to its description. You can pass the same policy to the Markdown renderer: `new Texy\Markdown\Output\Markdown\Renderer($safe->urlPolicy)`.

